{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.MassTheory.ReebPnMagnitude","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.MassTheory.ReebPnMagnitude","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Matrix.Notation","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Ring.Units","lean:module:Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Matrix.Determinant.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Matrix.Trace","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.LinearCombination","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring"],"declarations":15,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.ReebPnMagnitude.pn_sign_trace_blind","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.ReebPnMagnitude.pn_sign_trace_blind","kind":"theorem","line":179,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/ReebPnMagnitude.lean#L179-L204","doc":"**pn-sign blindness of charge-diagonal observables** — for any operator\n`T` commuting with the p/n grading `D` (every charge-diagonal / isospin-\ndiagonal observable does), all mixed traces are pn-sign invariant:\n`tr(T · S(a, w)^k) = tr(T · S(a, −w)^k)`.\n\nConsequence: the [1⁴] pn writhe sign is invisible not just to the\ndeterminant (`pn_sign_det_invariant`) but to the ENTIRE spectral/mixed-trace\nobservable class built from grading-commuting operators.  Sign-sensitivity\nrequires an operator that fails to commute with `D` — i.e. one that mixes\nthe p and n blocks, which in the framework is the weak/β sector (isospin\nraising/lowering).  The \"open pn sign\" of PR #3426 is therefore a\nweak-sector observable, not a binding-sector one. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.ReebPnMagnitude.pn_sign_conjugation_pow","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.ReebPnMagnitude.pn_sign_conjugation_pow","kind":"theorem","line":173,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/ReebPnMagnitude.lean#L173-L177","doc":"`pn_sign_conjugation` extended to powers:\n`S(a, −w)^k = D · S(a, w)^k · D`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.ReebPnMagnitude.conj_pow","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.ReebPnMagnitude.conj_pow","kind":"theorem","line":161,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/ReebPnMagnitude.lean#L161-L171","doc":"Conjugation by an involution distributes over powers:\n`(D M D)^k = D M^k D` whenever `D * D = 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.ReebPnMagnitude.pn_sign_det_invariant","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.ReebPnMagnitude.pn_sign_det_invariant","kind":"theorem","line":144,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/ReebPnMagnitude.lean#L144-L159","doc":"**pn-sign isospectrality for determinant-class observables** —\n`det(1 + S(a, w)) = det(1 + S(a, −w))`.  Since `D² = 1` and\n`det D = 1`, `1 + S(a, −w) = D (1 + S(a, w)) D` and the determinant is\nconjugation-invariant.  Consequence: the \"open pn sign\" (PR #3426) is\nunobservable in any `−ln det(I + G)`-class binding form at uniform\nper-pair magnitude. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.ReebPnMagnitude.pn_sign_conjugation","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.ReebPnMagnitude.pn_sign_conjugation","kind":"theorem","line":136,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/ReebPnMagnitude.lean#L136-L142","doc":"**pn-sign conjugation** — `D · S(a, w) · D = S(a, −w)`: the parity\nconjugator flips every pn entry and fixes the pp/nn entries. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.ReebPnMagnitude.parityD_sq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.ReebPnMagnitude.parityD_sq","kind":"theorem","line":131,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/ReebPnMagnitude.lean#L131-L134","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.ReebPnMagnitude.parityD","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.ReebPnMagnitude.parityD","kind":"def","line":126,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/ReebPnMagnitude.lean#L126-L129","doc":"The parity conjugator `D = diag(1, 1, −1, −1)` (proton block `+`,\nneutron block `−` — the p↔n bar grading). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.ReebPnMagnitude.exchangeMatrix","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.ReebPnMagnitude.exchangeMatrix","kind":"def","line":118,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/ReebPnMagnitude.lean#L118-L124","doc":"The uniform-magnitude [1⁴] complete-graph exchange matrix on blocks\n`[p, p, n, n]`: pp entry `a`, nn entry `−a`, all four pn entries `w`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.ReebPnMagnitude.ss_det_no_linear_term","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.ReebPnMagnitude.ss_det_no_linear_term","kind":"theorem","line":96,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/ReebPnMagnitude.lean#L96-L116","doc":"**Open-bond vs closed-loop dichotomy** — the closed-loop (determinant)\nbinding of a single exchange kills the linear term.  For a 2×2 matrix the\ndeterminant expands as `det(1 + g·M) = 1 + g·tr M + g²·det M`; since the\nss-sector `M(b)` is traceless (`ssMatrix_trace_zero`), the **linear-in-`g`\ncoefficient vanishes** and the loop binding is `O(g²)`:\n`det(1 + g·M(b)) = 1 + g²·det M(b)`.\n\nThis is the structural reason the determinant (closed-loop) form\n**under-binds** the deuteron (`−E₀·ln(1−g²) ≈ 0.93` vs AME `2.22`): a single\n*open* pn bond contributes to the binding at first order in the crossing\namplitude `g`, but the trace-free loop object has no first-order term.  The\ndeuteron is one open pn bond, so its binding is the **linear** `O(g)` object\n(the reeb-flow `5g` law), not the quadratic loop determinant — which is why\nthe linear form is the physically correct one for the single pair.  (The\nresidual open question is the MeV unit of that linear object, not its\n`g`-order: see the audit §2h.) ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.ReebPnMagnitude.ssMatrix_trace_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.ReebPnMagnitude.ssMatrix_trace_zero","kind":"theorem","line":92,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/ReebPnMagnitude.lean#L92-L94","doc":"The ss-sector exchange matrix is **traceless**: `tr M(b) = 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.ReebPnMagnitude.ss_det_zero_at_criticality","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.ReebPnMagnitude.ss_det_zero_at_criticality","kind":"theorem","line":81,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/ReebPnMagnitude.lean#L81-L90","doc":"**Marginal stability** — at the deformed criticality\n`g²·(1 + b²) = 1` (the landed magnitude, by I1/I2 with `b = [2]_v`) the\nss-sector determinant vanishes exactly: `det(1 + g·M) = 0`.  The landed\n`g_reeb` is the marginal-stability point of the q-deformed exchange — the\nalgebraic counterpart of the deuteron being marginally bound.  (Below\ncriticality the determinant is positive; the AME value sits on the bound\nside, `B_AME < 5g`, consistent in sign.) ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:prop:reeb-pn-magnitude-criticality","label":"prop:reeb-pn-magnitude-criticality","md":"quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/reeb-pn-magnitude-criticality.md"}],"signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.ReebPnMagnitude.ss_det_marginal","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.ReebPnMagnitude.ss_det_marginal","kind":"theorem","line":74,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/ReebPnMagnitude.lean#L74-L79","doc":"The ss-sector determinant is affine in `g²`:\n`det(1 + g·M(b)) = 1 − g²·(1 + b²)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.ReebPnMagnitude.ss_matrix_sq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.ReebPnMagnitude.ss_matrix_sq","kind":"theorem","line":65,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/ReebPnMagnitude.lean#L65-L72","doc":"**I2** (Cayley–Hamilton form) — the traceless ss-sector matrix squares to\nthe scalar `1 + b²`.  At `b = 2` (classical pn degree) the scalar is `5`\n(the `√5` ceiling); at `b = [2]_v` it is the deformed radicand of I1. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.ReebPnMagnitude.ssMatrix","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.ReebPnMagnitude.ssMatrix","kind":"def","line":61,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/ReebPnMagnitude.lean#L61-L63","doc":"The 2×2 ss-sector exchange matrix `[[1, b], [b, −1]]`:\n`1` is the pp−nn bar asymmetry, `b` the (quantum-counted) pn degree. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.ReebPnMagnitude.deformation_eq_ceiling","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.ReebPnMagnitude.deformation_eq_ceiling","kind":"theorem","line":51,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/ReebPnMagnitude.lean#L51-L59","doc":"**I1** — the deformation identity: `5 + (v − v⁻¹)² = 1 + (v + v⁻¹)²`\nfor any invertible `v`.  It is `(a+b)² − (a−b)² = 4ab` with `ab = 1`;\nwith `v = √q` the left side is `5 + (q−1)²/q` and the right side is\n`1 + [2]_v²` — the landed pn-magnitude radicand equals the q-deformed\nss-ceiling eigenvalue squared. ","signatureChunk":0}]}